## 7. Source Inventory and Import Manifest

Introduce a lightweight source manifest.

The manifest should support:

- Stable source reference ID.
- Original repository.
- Exact source path.
- Source title.
- Source commit or tree revision, when verified.
- Source file hash, when available.
- Document classification.
- Expected content type.
- Associated project phase.
- Import status.
- Publication authorization status.
- Published Knowledge Hub content ID.
- Completeness classification.
- Available languages.
- Source verification status.

Suggested import statuses:

- Expected.
- Awaiting Source.
- Source Received.
- Validated.
- Imported.
- Published.
- Rejected.

Keep source import status separate from official ERP approval status.

Do not create a complex workflow engine for the import manifest.

A validated JSON or TypeScript-based manifest is sufficient.

The manifest must make it possible to distinguish:

- How many source documents exist.
- How many have been received.
- How many have been imported.
- How many are publicly available.

Only actual imported and authorized content may contribute to published document counts.

